# 27. Three-Tier Application Example

A realistic architecture:

```text
                    Internet
                       │
                       ▼
                 ┌───────────┐
                 │  Ingress  │
                 └─────┬─────┘
                       │
                       ▼
              ┌─────────────────┐
              │    Frontend     │
              │   Pods          │
              └────────┬────────┘
                       │
                  TCP 8080
                       │
                       ▼
              ┌─────────────────┐
              │     Backend     │
              │      Pods       │
              └────────┬────────┘
                       │
                  TCP 27017
                       │
                       ▼
              ┌─────────────────┐
              │     MongoDB     │
              │      Pods       │
              └─────────────────┘
```

Desired security model:

```text
Internet → Frontend        ✅
Frontend → Backend         ✅
Backend → MongoDB          ✅

Internet → MongoDB         ❌
Frontend → MongoDB         ❌
Other Pods → MongoDB       ❌
```

NetworkPolicies can help enforce these communication boundaries.

---

# 28. NetworkPolicy Security Model

A common approach is:

```text
1. Start with default deny
           ↓
2. Identify required communication
           ↓
3. Allow only required traffic
           ↓
4. Test application
           ↓
5. Monitor and refine policies
```

This is a practical implementation of **least-privilege networking**.

---

# 29. NetworkPolicy vs Firewall

NetworkPolicy and traditional firewalls are related but operate at different layers and scopes.

| NetworkPolicy                          | Traditional Firewall                |
| -------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| Kubernetes-native                      | Network/infrastructure-level        |
| Primarily Pod traffic                  | Hosts, networks, interfaces, etc.   |
| Uses Pod/Namespace selectors and CIDRs | Often IPs, ports, interfaces, zones |
| Managed as Kubernetes objects          | Usually managed separately          |
| Depends on CNI enforcement             | Depends on firewall technology      |

They can complement each other rather than being mutually exclusive.

