# 🎯 Final Takeaway

The main concept I learned is:

> **NetworkPolicy allows Kubernetes administrators to define which network connections are permitted between Pods, namespaces, and selected IP ranges.**

Instead of allowing unrestricted Pod-to-Pod communication, NetworkPolicies can be used to implement a **least-privilege network model** where applications communicate only with the components they actually need.

A practical pattern is:

```text
Default Deny
     ↓
Explicit Allow
     ↓
Least-Privilege Networking
     ↓
Better Pod Isolation
     ↓
Improved Kubernetes Security
```

NetworkPolicy is therefore an important part of securing Kubernetes workloads and building **segmented, zero-trust-oriented application architectures**.

